<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>FIR No. 251/2020, PS North Rohini, was registered on the complainant's allegation that the accused and her relatives obstructed and assaulted him on 8 May 2020 and took his gold chain and purse — offences under Sections 188, 323, 341, 379 and 34 IPC, with Section 506 added later. The complainant and the accused had previously been engaged.</p>
<p>In September 2021 the accused claimed juvenility on the strength of a school leaving certificate recording her date of birth as 1 November 2003. A Magistrate declared her a child in conflict with law; the Sessions Court set that aside in June 2022 and remanded for a fresh inquiry; and on 27 September 2022 the Magistrate, after the Investigating Officer verified records from two schools, again declared her a child with the same date of birth. The complainant's challenge to that finding eventually reached the High Court in revision.</p>

<h2>School records versus marriage records</h2>
<p>The complainant's counter-case rested on documents giving the accused a date of birth of 1 November 1999: Arya Samaj Mandir records, a custody warrant, a 2021 marriage deed and a 2021 marriage certificate. The Court's analysis turned on chronology and the statutory hierarchy:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Statutory preference.</strong> Section 94(2)(i) of the JJ Act gives first preference to the date of birth certificate from the school. The school records here — from 2010, 2011 and 2015, across three schools — consistently showed 1 November 2003.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Pre-dispute provenance.</strong> Those records predate the 2020 dispute, and rest on affidavits of the accused's mother (2010) and father (2011). The complainant could point to no deficiency in them; the fabrication allegation was unsubstantiated.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Post-dispute documents deferred.</strong> The 1999-date documents were created in 2020-21. The Court held the complainant may rely on them in the separate FIR No. 626/2021, where any relief will be decided.</div>
</div>

<h2>Two procedural rulings</h2>
<p>First, on forum: the Court rejected the argument that only the Juvenile Justice Board could determine juvenility. Section 9(2) of the JJ Act expressly contemplates an age inquiry by a court other than the Board when the claim is raised before it, and Section 8(1)'s grant of exclusive jurisdiction to the Board is subject to the Act's other provisions.</p>
<p>Second, on participation: the complainant demanded cross-examination of the school principals and record keepers. Applying <em>Rekha Murarka v. State of West Bengal</em>, (2020) 2 SCC 474, the Court held that a complainant has no vested right to cross-examine at an age inquiry where the prosecution is satisfied with the verified documents — the inquiry under Section 94 is not a mini-trial.</p>
