<h2>Takeaways for practitioners</h2>
<p>Age challenges fail or succeed on the Section 94 hierarchy. A party attacking a school record must show a concrete deficiency in that record — not merely produce later documents pointing the other way, especially documents generated after the litigation began. Conversely, a party supporting juvenility should prove the school record through the issuing institution and trace it to its earliest source, as the Investigating Officer's verification across two schools did here. The second revision (against the appellate order terminating proceedings before the Board) fell automatically once the main challenge failed — a reminder to structure connected revisions so that each has an independent footing where possible.</p>
<div class="note">Related coverage: the hierarchy of age-proof under Section 94 also governs POCSO prosecutions, where the victim's age is an ingredient of the offence rather than a question of forum.</div>
